Asymptotic notation is a mathematical tool used to describe the efficiency of algorithms as the input size grows—especially when that size becomes very large. It helps computer scientists and engineers analyze how an algorithm performs in terms of time or space, without getting bogged down by machine-specific constants or small input quirks.

Here’s a breakdown of the main types of asymptotic notation:

📈 Big O Notation (O)

Describes the worst-case scenario

Represents the upper bound on time or space complexity

Example: Searching in an unsorted list → O(n)

📉 Omega Notation (Ω)

Describes the best-case scenario

Represents the lower bound on performance

Example: If an algorithm always takes at least n steps → Ω(n)

⚖️ Theta Notation (Θ)

Describes the average-case or tight bound

Represents both upper and lower bounds

Example: If an algorithm always takes exactly n steps → Θ(n)

🐢 Little o Notation (o)

Represents a non-tight upper bound

Means the algorithm grows strictly slower than another function

Example: f(n) = o(n²) implies f(n) grows slower than n²

🐇 Little omega Notation (ω)

Represents a non-tight lower bound

Means the algorithm grows strictly faster than another function

Example: f(n) = ω(n) implies f(n) grows faster than linear

🧠 Why It Matters

Helps compare algorithms regardless of hardware

Focuses on scalability and long-term performance

Essential for choosing the right algorithm for large datasets


